Z||000000||88formula||10-28-1999||08:46 PM||||I just bought an awesome 88 formula, and with the purchase I got some brochures that are for the 88 Pontiac Fiero. In one of those 88 pontiac brochures called the "Hot Ones" it list performance specifications for all the 88 model year pontiac cars including the fiero. These  are the specifications that it lists, <BR>0-60..................8.7 SEC<BR>1/4 Mile..............17.1 SEC<BR>Braking 60-0...............186 ft<BR>Lateral acceleration,g's...0.86 <BR>weight/horspower ratio.....20.4:1<BR>(The car tested was an 88 GT w/2.8l engine, 3.61:1 axile ratio and 5-speed manual).<BR>At the end it says "all performance figures obtained with professional driver on General Motors closed test area". <BR>I've visited a lot of websites looking for stock performance statisics on this sweet looking car and found performance statistics ranging from, <BR> 0-60..................7.1-8.5 SEC<BR>1/4 Mile..............15.5-17.1 SEC<BR>Braking 60-0...............135-186 ft<BR>Lateral acceleration,g's...0.84-0.88 <P>According to this brochure the car dose not brake and accelerate as well as everyone wants to believe. Im not bashing v6 fieros here, I just want to know why these figures are so optimistic as compared to the factory performance statisics. Whats your thoughts on this fiero enthusiasts||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cooter||10-28-1999||09:04 PM||||Well, one night my dad raced my best friend coming back from RC car racing. We were in a "borrowed" 4 door civic, Rick was in his CRX Si. All the racing equipment was in the 4 door. When the light went green, my dad blew the CRX away. They then swapped cars and my dad in the CRX blew away the civic. The driver will make the difference in a lot of cars, especially stick shifts. This is when I learned that you could do full throttle upshifts and have been doing ever since.||||reg||3
Z||000002||superXmotorsports||10-28-1999||11:27 PM||||I owned an 87 GT with a hypertech chip, K&N filter w/ scoop, and muffler-eliminator exhaust.  I ran a 15.1 @ 92mph.  Stock it ran 15.65 @ 89 mph. just FYI.  Anybody else?<BR>(My 87 GT w/ 3.2 5spd runs 14.44 @ 102)||||reg||4
Z||000003||88formula||10-29-1999||08:52 PM||||What do you mien by full throttle upshifts? ||||reg||1
Z||000004||sinoloa||10-29-1999||09:59 PM||||a full throttle upshift is when you have a stick shift car and whenever you shift the gears, always hold your accelerator to the floor even when changing gears.  This is done when drag racing.  You still use your clutch to change gears, you just never let your foot off the accelerator.||||reg||10
Z||000005||hefty||10-29-1999||11:56 PM||||I love rowing thru the gears, I absolutly pegged the 1-2 shift against a new camero that was reving his engine, jumped so far ahead I banged third and let off, It sounded louder than stock and at the next light I tried to ask if it was a stock 6. Dude wouldn't acknowledge me !||||reg||7
Z||000006||Eric||10-30-1999||11:46 AM||||The braking is way off.  It should be down around 120 from 60-0.  186ft would be like 250 from 70.  That's worse than almost any car made in the last 10 years.  A Hummer brakes from 70 in 241 feet.  You should disregard any performace tests with "information" like this.<P>Realistically, a new 88GT should get to 60 in about 8 seconds and through the quarter in 16 flat.  Lateral acceleration should be around .90 g's with good tires and shocks.||||reg||1
Z||000007||88formula||10-30-1999||10:29 PM|||| Eric, maybe the brochure I have here from GM is just full of typos. In this book it lists other cars 60-0 braking such as Grand Am  172.7ft, Firbird 139ft, Grand Prix 170ft, BONNEVILLE, 144.5FT, 6000STE 160ft, Subird 174.9ft, Lemans 139ft. I have an 89 Bonneville, and I  think it stops better than my 88 formula, but on the flip side my fiero suffers no brake fade and my bonneville after repeated stops will.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||10-30-1999||10:45 PM||||Just finished skimming my Motorweek Fiero video, here are their results:<BR>all stops are 55mph to 0-<BR>1984: 125 ft<BR>1985 with v-6: 130 ft<BR>1988 with v-6: 118 ft<P>86 and 87 don't have this test on the video. probly the same though.  <P>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 10-30-1999).]<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 10-31-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||88formula||10-31-1999||05:17 PM||||Look in your owners manual under braking and than stopping distances and tell me what you see, the facts, not what should be. The 360hp 95,000 dollar Porsche 911GT3 with four wheel cross drilled vented discs stops in 111ft, thats only 17ft less than 118 for the 14,000 in 88 fiero, WOW!||||reg||4
Z||000010||fiero56||10-31-1999||05:47 PM||||The '88 they tested was a Formula.  It was a comparison between the '88 Fiero and the MR2.  The video said there was virtually no brake fade and that they were impressed with the stopping power of the '88.  I'm going up stairs now to dig out my owners manual.  I'll post what it says.||||reg||1
Z||000011||fiero56||10-31-1999||05:57 PM||||My owners manual says from 60 to 0 takes 186 feet.  The video was from 55 to 0.  There will be a difference in stopping distances due to different speeds, but I doubt it would be this much.  I personally am not going to believe what this manual says about braking.  (Chart looks too generic if you ask me.)  Anyone have a manual from '84 to '87. I'm sure they'll say the same distance, but braking was improved for '88.  It's on the video.  Call up the Fiero Store and order it, you'll see the Fiero stop in 118 feet.<BR>On the Porsche stopping distance note: you could spen a million dollars, but once you get to a certain point, no matter what you do to the car, it won't stop any shorter, no matter what technology you put on.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 10-31-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||88formula||10-31-1999||07:06 PM||||OK, I will have to check that out, I think you have a good point, thanks.<P>Sinoloa, about that full throttle shifting, that must be how everyone came up with these low 0-60 time for their fieros. QUESTION won't that kind of shifting really shorten the life of your clutch? When you push in the clutch to shift gears with the throttle floored, won't your rpm dangerously rocket to the redline and possibly blow the engine before you can engage the next gear? This car (88 Formula) is my first stick. <p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 10-31-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||fiero56||10-31-1999||10:24 PM||||To get those 0-60 times on the 4-cyl, they did full throttle shifts.  I was amazed, tires squeeling off the line, a good loud "chirp"(almost a squeal) when they hit second, was pretty impressive.  I don't think my clutch is fluid enough for fast enough shifts without over-reving the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Eric||11-01-1999||06:20 PM||||If you're only going to 60mph, the full throttle shifts won't do you much good.  It may take off a tenth or two at most.  Quarter mile may be a different story, but  I don't think it would give you much there either since the object is to put power to the ground not waste it in wheelspin.  Personally I don't think it's worth the risk.  All it really does is make you look cool in front of your friends when you chirp the tires in second.  ||||reg||1
Z||000015||hefty||11-01-1999||11:54 PM||||I think the key is shifting at optimum RPM, and to keep the rpm in the power range ( not letting rpm drop below say 3-3500k )when you bang the next gear. You still need an equal exchange on gas and clutch, you just pound the clutch fast, and lift and hammer the gas. One thing I've learned, if you want the most out of your car, drive it like you hate it. Speed kills, and I'm talking drivetrains !<p>[This message has been edited by hefty (edited 11-01-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Cooter||11-02-1999||08:59 PM||||When I was into playing the stop-light grand prix games, I would really dog my poor little civic. Launch at 6500 rpms and full throttle upshift at 7800. I did not worry about over revving the engine because it shuts down at 7800 so if I missed a shift, it was not going any higher anyway. I did not impress my friends by chirping the tires in second. I pissed off people by chirping the tires in third. Some of those who saw civic tail lights were a couple of mitsubishi 3000's, a few late model vettes, a big block 68 camaro, 4.6 mustangs and more plastic firebirds and camaros than I care to count. Yes, this is hard on the clutch. It was replaced at 60,000 miles and now grinds a little when shifting into second too fast. The car has 140,000 miles on it, so I dont treat it that bad any more. Getting pulled over on the way home from PTO meetings does not make teachers look very professional, so I gave up the games. Maybe one day I will have a hot rod Fiero to take to the track and play.||||reg||1
Z||000017||88formula||11-03-1999||08:43 PM||||Why dose the manufacturer post the 88 fieros stopping distance as 186ft from 60mph and motorweek says it can do it in 118ft, thats a 68-foot difference?||||reg||1
Z||000019||fiero56||11-03-1999||10:04 PM||||Ok, I was too lazy to do this before but here it is.  I just took a picture of my TV with Motorweek doing the Fiero test.  Here it is:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1_2.jpg"> <P>And now finally, the actuall test:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/3_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000020||fiero56||11-03-1999||10:10 PM||||Like I said, the video was from 55 to 0.  So it should be a little longer if your going from 60, but nothing like 186ft.<BR>Looks like I cut off the bottom of the screen a little bit, but everyone should be able to read it.  I still don't think those owners manuals for the braking distance are model/year specific.  I think GM just xeroxed a braking bar graph for whatever class the Fiero fell in.  I don't think it reflects the Fiero's abilities.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 11-03-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||Eric||11-04-1999||05:01 PM||||Why doesn't someone try the 60-0 test.  If my 88GT had an engine inside it, I would do it in a second.  I'd just go to a deserted road, find a mailbox or some reference point, go to the point at sixty and jump on the brakes making sure the don't lock.  Then I'd mark the point where it stoped and pace it off.  It would be too easy. ||||reg||1
